WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.876
Course graphics should explain something.

2
00:00:02.876 --> 00:00:14.224
Useful eLearning graphics include process flows, comparison charts, checklists, visual summaries, concept maps, decision trees, and diagrams that show relationships.

3
00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:21.142
A graphic that only fills space may make a screen look complete, but it does not necessarily help learning.

4
00:00:21.142 --> 00:00:26.582
The best graphics earn their place by making a difficult idea easier to understand.

5
00:00:26.582 --> 00:00:30.236
A practical production choice is raster versus vector.

6
00:00:30.236 --> 00:00:37.619
Raster graphics are pixel-based and are commonly used for photos, screenshots, textures, and detailed images.

7
00:00:37.619 --> 00:00:47.024
Vector graphics are shape-based and can often scale cleanly, which makes them useful for icons, diagrams, line art, and simple illustrations.

8
00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:51.299
The format should match the use case and the expected screen size.

9
00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:52.854
Export formats matter.

10
00:00:52.854 --> 00:00:57.129
PNG is often useful for crisp screen graphics with transparency.

11
00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:01.870
JPG may be appropriate for photographic images where file size matters.

12
00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:07.700
SVG can be useful for scalable vector artwork when the delivery environment supports it.

13
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.809
PDF can work well for downloadable references.

14
00:01:10.809 --> 00:01:17.571
These are practical choices, not universal rules, and they should be tested in the course environment.

15
00:01:17.571 --> 00:01:22.468
Consistent templates and reusable components improve quality and speed.

16
00:01:22.468 --> 00:01:30.396
A course may use a standard diagram style, repeated icon treatment, consistent callout boxes, and predictable spacing.

17
00:01:30.396 --> 00:01:36.070
Reuse helps learners recognize patterns, and it helps designers update assets later.

18
00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:42.443
Consistency should not make every screen identical, but it should make the course feel coherent.

19
00:01:42.443 --> 00:01:44.930
Design graphics for the destination.

20
00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:51.848
A 16:9 slide, a responsive lesson block, and a downloadable handout may require different compositions.

21
00:01:51.848 --> 00:01:54.568
Text inside a graphic must stay readable.

22
00:01:54.568 --> 00:01:56.900
Diagrams should survive resizing.

23
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:03.196
A visual summary should remain useful outside the course if learners download it as a reference.

24
00:02:03.196 --> 00:02:07.471
Production begins with understanding where the asset will live.
